WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.969
Film Courage: What are three key points every
filmmaker should know about getting a movie

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:04.969
made?

00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:05.969
Jeff Deverett, Film Producer, Entrepreneur,
Writer, Director: I would say Point Number

00:00:05.969 --> 00:00:11.250
1 - and this is remember I’m talking about
a filmmaker who has the intention to make

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.550
money to sell their film because if their
intention is just to create a movie that is

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.240
artistic that they want to finish and put
on their shelf and enjoy that’s not what

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.430
I’m talking about.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:26.199
So if we’re talking about somebody who makes
a movie with the intention of making a profit

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:33.610
on it (selling it and hopefully making a profit)
the first thing they should do they have to

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.780
think about who they are going to sell it
to, who the audience is, who they are making

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.550
the movie for, that’s number one.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.500
Number two is…so number one is identifying
the audience.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.280
Number two is - how are they going to market
to that audience?

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:56.610
So now they know what audience they want to
get to but how are they going to get there?

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:02.230
What publicity campaign, what social media
campaign, whatever are they going to use to

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.430
get to that audience?

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:10.250
Now you might be thinking to yourself you
asked me about making a movie and I’m talking

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.860
about selling a movie.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.800
I honestly believe that if you want to make
money with your movie as I would say for any

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.860
busy or any product (not just a movie), you’ve
got to be thinking about your audience and

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.700
how to market them before you even make your
movie.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.740
And then number three would be - how to deploy
your funds as effectively as possible so you’re

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.500
getting as we talked about before the most
of your money on screen.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.610
So you’re making the best product possible.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:41.310
Don’t waste your time and make a crappy
product, you’ll regret it and it won’t

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.310
sell.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.720
So make something that you’re going to be
proud of that you feel like you’re going

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.360
to have a shot to sell but before you do that
make sure you know who you’re making it

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.390
for and you have an avenue to get to them
whether it be through a distributor or through

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:00.299
your own means or through your own friends
or whatever marketing you’re going to do.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:06.909
Now I tell people this, this is where indie
filmmakers really, really struggle with what

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:09.260
I am about to say and this is what I say to
them.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:19.050
I say when you’re budgeting for movie make
sure that if you want to be successful, if

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:26.650
you’re going to spend $500,000 on making
your movie, spend at least…at least $300,000

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.990
on marketing it.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:36.459
You have to have between 60 and 100% of your
production budget you also have to have for

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.010
a marketing budget and they say that’s crazy.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.010
How are we going to do that?

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.209
We can hardly raise enough money to make the
movie.

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:46.849
How are we going to raise money to market
the movie?

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:52.220
And I say if you want to have a shot, like
if you want to compare yourself to Hollywood

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.890
movies and studio movies look at what they
do.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:03:00.819
I mean every single movie they put out has
a gigantic marketing budget.

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:05.370
In some cases it’s the production budget
and the marketing budget are the same even

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.180
on 50 million dollar movie.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.319
The marketing budget might be more actually.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:16.480
That’s what makes it successful because
studios understand that it’s one thing to

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.660
make a great movie but it’s nothing unless
you can get it in front of an audience and

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.640
you have to advertise it to them.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.590
Indie filmmakers don’t necessarily do that.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:29.540
So remember the two things that I led with
were know who your audience is and make sure

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:35.400
you have the wherewithal to get to them which
usually involves resources so make sure that

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:41.580
when you’re budgeting you tuck a big chunk
of money aside for marketing.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.180
That’s funny I’ve listen to a lot of your
webcasts here and a lot of filmmakers talk

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.000
about it and they talk about make sure you
have enough money and most people say “Make

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.140
sure you have enough money for post-production.”

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.350
And I listen to those and I say “Are you
kidding me?”

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:59.090
Like post-production is part of making the
movie, you actually went and shot a film and

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.220
didn’t think about post-production?

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.280
That’s crazy.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.070
To me it’s crazy, like I would never do
that.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:09.940
I would never go into production without having
enough money to finish the movie because what’s

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.390
the point of that.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:16.989
Secondly some people who are a little further
along say “Make sure you have enough for

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:17.989
delivery.”

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:20.009
Because it’s expensive to deliver.

00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:23.360
You know there are elements you have to do
to deliver.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.939
I also say of course you have to deliver.

00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:29.259
That’s part of the production budget.

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:32.970
But how many indie filmmakers ever say make
sure you have enough to market?

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.199
And it’s painful to say that, that’s actually
painful because if you have an extra quarter

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:41.770
of a million dollars and you’re sitting
and you know it’s tucked away there for

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.240
marketing, you’re inclined to spend it on
the movie.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.659
I’ll make a way better movie if I have quarter
of a million dollars more think of how I could

00:04:48.659 --> 00:04:52.659
spend it, think of how good the movie could
be but then you’ve got nothing left to market

00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:53.659
it with.

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:57.330
So no matter how good a movie you’ve made,
you can’t market it.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.539
Now here is what you’ll ask me and here
is what everybody says but that’s the distributors

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:04.800
job to market it, it’s not my job to market
it, it’s the distributor.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.159
So I’m going to get a distributor and they’re
going to spend a quarter of a million dollars

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:09.159
to market it.

00:05:09.159 --> 00:05:12.949
That’s what they do and I say maybe.

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:16.479
That means first of all you’ve got to get
lucky and get a distributor.

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:19.719
Secondly there aren’t too many distributors
these days that are going to spend a quarter

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:22.710
of a million dollars marketing an indie movie.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:29.180
So if you really, really want to get behind
your movie and you really believe in it and

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:34.939
you want to have a shot at getting it successful
tuck that money aside, raise extra money,

00:05:34.939 --> 00:05:36.009
and tell your investors.

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:37.729
They will actually respect that.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:41.409
They will respect the fact that you’re actually
thinking about marketing as much as you’re

00:05:41.409 --> 00:05:43.090
thinking about production.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:49.680
Tuck it aside and if you do get a distributor,
if you do get lucky and get a good distributor

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.849
have them deploy those resources on your behalf
so that they have a war chest to help get

00:05:55.849 --> 00:05:56.849
the movie out.

00:05:56.849 --> 00:05:58.490
It’s only going to be to your benefit.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.849
There are some tricks on how to make that
happen so that they don’t beat you and take

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:04.190
fees on your own money and take costs and
all that kind of stuff.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:06.809
We will have to talk about that at some point.

00:06:06.809 --> 00:06:10.780
So don’t just throw it in their laps because
they will spend it and you won’t get any

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:13.180
return on it.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:18.189
And the other thing is if you don’t get
a distributor, you may have to self-distribute

00:06:18.189 --> 00:06:22.550
and if you’re going to self-distribute you
need to have some resources to do that with.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:27.840
It’s not good enough to throw out just a
crappy social media campaign these days.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:28.960
It’s too crowded out there.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.249
Nobody is going to see it.

00:06:30.249 --> 00:06:31.610
You have to spend some money.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.949
Film Courage: Well what about cream rises
to the top.

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:38.960
So if I get it on one of these streaming services,
I don’t need to.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.659
Don’t worry, it will be suggested.

00:06:41.659 --> 00:06:47.029
Jeff: That’s the typical thing if I get
it on a streaming service.

00:06:47.029 --> 00:06:50.240
Do you know what the odds are of getting it
on a streaming service?

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:58.150
That would be like if I get into Sundance
then a big company will see it and I will…let’s

00:06:58.150 --> 00:06:59.150
talk about Sundance.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:00.550
That’s okay, that’s like the streaming
company.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.460
It’s the same type of deal, right?

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:07.080
Getting an indie movie into Sundance now is
almost impossible.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.970
I mean the acceptance rate is so low.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.840
It’s like getting accepted to UCLA for college
students.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.729
Like 114,000 applications and 3,000 spots.

00:07:16.729 --> 00:07:19.439
It’s extremely, extremely low.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:24.069
So the chances of you getting accepted into
Sundance with your indie film are very, very

00:07:24.069 --> 00:07:25.069
low.

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:28.449
But let’s say you’re one of the lucky
ones and you actually do get it in, all right.

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:33.460
That’s not enough because the next thing
you have to do is you have to get a screening

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:38.960
time, it’s about getting the screening where
buyers will actually come.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.620
So I am using Sundance because it’s one
of the major festivals and we know that there

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:46.339
are lots of acquisition executives and movie
buyers who could make a difference, who could

00:07:46.339 --> 00:07:48.729
make your movie but it has to be seen.

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:52.699
You have to spend a fortune, you have to hire
a publicist.

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:54.939
First of all, you have to hopefully get a
good screening time.

00:07:54.939 --> 00:07:59.179
If it’s nine in the morning and there are
parties the night before, nobody is coming

00:07:59.179 --> 00:08:00.179
to your screening.

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:01.639
You gave to get an afternoon and I mean that’s
hard to do, right?

00:08:01.639 --> 00:08:04.360
You’ve got to get lucky with that.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:05.373
Now you get that so now you’ve gotten lucky
twice.

00:08:05.373 --> 00:08:12.699
You have to hire a publicity team and a promotion
team, you can’t just assume people are going

00:08:12.699 --> 00:08:13.699
to come.

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:15.089
Somebody has got to be talking it up.

00:08:15.089 --> 00:08:19.279
That costs a lot of money to hire those people
especially at Sundance where it’s super

00:08:19.279 --> 00:08:21.790
competitive and where it’s hard to get the
attention of the buyers because they only

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:23.610
want to see the big movies.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:29.509
So that’s…when I say a lot of money that
could be anywhere from $5,000 to $10,000 minimum,

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:30.699
minimum.

00:08:30.699 --> 00:08:33.940
Not to mention you have to be at Sundance
and spend money to be there and all of that

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:34.940
kind of stuff.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.349
So now you get the buyers there.

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:41.261
Likely your publicity person will say you
have to have a party because afterwards you

00:08:41.261 --> 00:08:44.770
have to entertain them and they have to schmooze
with you and blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:49.030
There’s another $10,000 to $20,000 maybe
down the toilet but this is what you’re

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:50.030
competing with.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:53.530
This is what studios do so it’s not like
everybody says “I’ll get into Sundance

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.150
and I’ll be set.”

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:58.560
And then you’ve got to hope that somebody
is in a good mood and liked your champagne

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.440
and it was expensive enough that they want
to make you an offer.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.330
So there is a lot that goes into it.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.410
Same with the streaming service, what you
think you pick up the phone and call Netflix

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:11.080
and say "I just made a great film” “That’s
great, send it over.”

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.460
It doesn’t work that way.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:18.950
You could wait years and years and years before
anybody even sees it or you can get lucky

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.590
but I don’t depend on luck.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:21.590
Question for the Viewers:

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:21.602
What are your thoughts on spending 40% of
your budget on marketing?

